course: dictionary
lesson: 16002
title: Valid: allowed, sensible, and correct
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: «Valid» has a few meanings, and the context tells you which one to use. «This train ticket is valid until Sunday.» Here valid means officially allowed and still able to be used, because the ticket has not run out.
explain: «Your idea is a valid point, so we will think about it.» Here valid means sensible and based on good reasons. The word «point» is the clue: someone has said something that deserves to be taken seriously.
explain: In maths and science, valid means the rules were followed correctly. «The conclusion is valid because every step was checked.» Ask yourself: is this about a ticket, a reason, or a rule? The clue words tell you which meaning fits.
fact: Valid means officially acceptable and able to be used.
fact: Valid can also mean sensible and based on good reasons, as in «a valid excuse».
fact: In science, a valid test changes only one thing, so the results really answer the question.
fact: Useful phrases include «valid for», «valid until», «a valid reason» and «a valid point».
fact: The opposite of valid is invalid, which means not allowed or not sound.
exercise: «This train ticket is valid until Sunday.» What does valid mean here? | Free to share with friends | Very old and worn out | *Officially allowed, and can still be used | Lost and never found
exercise: Maya says, «That is a valid point.» What does valid mean in this sentence? | Officially allowed to be used | Correct only in maths | Loud and clear | *Sensible and based on good reasons
exercise: In a fair science test, what must be true? | The results are never written down | *Only one thing changes, so the results answer the question | Several things change at once | Every test has a different question
test: «My passport is valid for another five years.» What does valid for mean here? | It costs five pounds | It is five years old | It must be shown five times | *It can still be used for five more years
test: «Your excuse is valid, so you can come in late.» What does a valid excuse mean? | A very long excuse | *A good reason that is accepted | A made-up story | An excuse said twice
exam: Which sentence uses valid to mean that a conclusion follows the rules correctly? | *«The conclusion is valid because every step was checked» | «The rain was valid all day» | «The coupon is valid until Monday» | «The dog looked valid and happy»
